  "articleBody": "Trying to fix a door frame yourself can seem straightforward, but there are definitely some pitfalls to steer clear of. One common mistake is repairing over rot or moisture damage without actually addressing the root cause. If you don't fix the leak or moisture issue first, that rot's just gonna come back, and you'll be doing the repair all over again. Another big one is skipping proper surface preparation; if you don't clean and sand the area well, your filler or paint won't adhere correctly, and it'll look shoddy pretty quickly. And trust me, it's tempting to rush, but not letting filler or glue fully cure before moving on is a recipe for disaster—it'll weaken the repair significantly. Finally, don't forget to re-check the frame's plumb, how the latch aligns, and whether the weather stripping seals properly after the repair. These little checks make sure your door works as it should, preventing future drafts or latch problems. For bigger jobs, like significant impact damage or full jamb replacements, it’s really smart to bring in a pro like HH Remodeling LLC here in Aurora, serving Denver and surrounding areas, to make sure it’s done right the first time.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
